# The Great Daufante — illusionist of Teppensteppe Hall

## Introduction

The Great Daufante is a wizard who makes even a short carriage ride feel like a grand theatrical entrance. He dresses in a high-collared robe of purple and blue, wears a decorated skull over the upper part of his face, and shows green eyes through its openings. A dark beard threaded with silver falls beneath the mask; gems circle the skull's brow. His pocket watch has no ordinary dial, but its face pulses in the direction of things he is seeking. With every flourish of fabric and conjured wind, he cultivates the impression of a man accustomed to both attention and obedience. His formal title invokes **Teppensteppe Hall**, although its exact form is not yet settled.[^entrance]

## A remarkable entourage

Daufante treats the undead with an affection he does not always show the living. His former assistant **Connor** continues to attend him as a reanimated corpse, wordless but able to follow orders, retrieve money and carry out awkward errands. The guest's treatment of guild driver Aegis is demanding: he insists that the centaur taste his tea before he drinks it. His **spider familiar Hamlet** is far more discreet, gathering whispers from the walls and servants' quarters. Daufante praises Hamlet's intelligence while scarcely considering the fear his zombie inspires in others.[^companions]

## The wedding and its secret

The wealthy Hooper family engages Daufante separately from three bronze-ranked adventurers to prevent a dead grandfather from disrupting a wedding. He is expected to conceal supernatural unpleasantness by illusion. Instead, the entertainer makes his own interests plain: magical texts and artifacts, he maintains, ought to belong to a wizard capable of wielding them. The Delvers object to his claim, but he accompanies Evelyn about the wedding grounds with impeccable manners, keeping Krandal and Koh under a rather less flattering appraisal.[^wedding]

With a few deceptive words and a spell of charm, he persuades elder Mr. Goodwine to confide a dreadful crime. In the trading wars of fifteen years earlier, Goodwine and his accomplices hanged the grandfather, leaving him among the trees. The grandfather has risen as a **wight**, with his noosed corpse hanging from a moving tree. Daufante hurls two volleys of star-bright missiles from his **Robe of Stars**, the second defeating the undead enemy. A blue spectral hand draws a **Deck of Many Things** from the creature's coat. Daufante neither touches the cards directly nor draws one; his interest is possession of the power they might confer.[^fight]

## Departure and current status

Daufante agrees to surrender his portion of the wedding reward to the Darkest Delvers in exchange for sole custody of the deck. He wraps the cards away in his **Bag of Holding** and departs the following day, his particular powers still as mysterious as his ambitions. Before going, he thanks his companions and offers Evelyn his address for a future evening at the theatre. His private interest in the paladin's divine circumstances is more calculating than the invitation suggests, but **no later appointment or alliance has occurred at this cutoff**. **Connor and Hamlet remain associated with him**, and there is no evidence that any card was drawn or activated.[^status]

[^entrance]: [Delvers S2E02](../../episodes/S2E02.md), ~00:03–00:08; **guest played by Bo Schwartz**, stage name rendered `Daufante/Daufonte/Dauphante` and hall title `Teppensteppe/Teppenstep Hall` pending audio/written confirmation. Pocket watch pulse is observed, its mechanics unverified.
[^companions]: [S2E02](../../episodes/S2E02.md), ~00:09–00:16, 00:32–00:38 and 00:56–01:07. Connor identified as dead former assistant, now animated; Hamlet a spider familiar gathering rumour, not eyewitness to the old killing.
[^wedding]: [S2E02](../../episodes/S2E02.md), ~00:23–00:40. Guest has **separate fee**, receives **270 gp upfront** as about half, other payments not individually reconciled; guild rank and actual full wizard class features not sheet-verified.
[^fight]: [S2E02](../../episodes/S2E02.md), ~02:04–02:37. Goodwine's murder confession is **NPC testimony under Charm Person**; wight and card deck later physically seen. Two Robe of Stars missile volleys performed; magic item details other than observed use not inferred.
[^status]: [S2E02](../../episodes/S2E02.md), ~02:36–02:47. Deck lifted by Mage Hand, packed without draw, fee/share waived, wizard departs next day; theatre invitation and guest player's hypothetical further plans not played.
